<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- Models page load time.

         ModelRepository.findAllWithPricing resolves, per model, the provider it
         was most recently routed to, so the catalog price it shows is the price
         of that provider:

             LEFT JOIN LATERAL (
                 SELECT le.provider_id FROM log_entry le
                 WHERE le.model_id = m.id AND le.provider_id IS NOT NULL
                 ORDER BY le.timestamp_request DESC LIMIT 1
             ) dp ON true

         A comment there says migration 014's (model_id, timestamp_request) makes
         this a single index seek. It does not, and measurement on production says
         so plainly: the whole query takes 9.6 s, of which 9.5 s is this lateral
         at 195 ms per model.

         The reason is that 014's index does not carry provider_id. With
         `provider_id IS NOT NULL` in the predicate, that index cannot answer the
         lateral on its own, so the planner instead picks
         idx_log_entry_performance_window — (timestamp_request DESC, provider_id,
         model_id) — which is covering but has model_id third. Equality on a
         non-leading column is not seekable, so each of the 49 models walks the
         index in timestamp order until a row for that model turns up; a model
         with no recent traffic walks all of it.

         This index puts model_id first, so the equality is the seek, keeps
         timestamp_request DESC next so LIMIT 1 stops at the first row, and
         carries provider_id as a payload so the scan stays index-only. The
         partial predicate matches the lateral's and keeps the index off every
         row that could never satisfy it.

         Measured on a production snapshot: 252 ms -> 1.7 ms for the full models
         query, with the lateral dropping from 5.1 ms to 0.004 ms per model.

         Runs outside a transaction so CONCURRENTLY can build without locking
         log_entry against the logos-orchestrator, which keeps writing to this
         table while logos-webservice is redeployed (same rationale as
         012/014/027/028/035). -->
    <changeSet id="036-log-entry-model-provider-recent-index" author="logos" runInTransaction="false">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><indexExists tableName="log_entry" indexName="idx_log_entry_model_provider_recent"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        <sql>
            CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_log_entry_model_provider_recent
                ON log_entry (model_id, timestamp_request DESC)
                INCLUDE (provider_id)
                WHERE provider_id IS NOT NULL;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF EXISTS idx_log_entry_model_provider_recent;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
